## AI-written code gets a human read

AI-generated code is more likely to carry security holes than code written slowly by hand.
After any big generation session:
- [ ] Read every line of login, permissions and access control code yourself.
- [ ] Check every place user input is shown on a page (cross-site scripting).
- [ ] Check that one user can never load another user's records by changing an id in a URL.
- [ ] Run `./scripts/security-check.sh`.

---

## Definition of done

A feature is not done until:
- [ ] `./scripts/security-check.sh` passes
- [ ] `./scripts/verify-quality.sh` passes
- [ ] Errors are handled, not swallowed
- [ ] AI-written auth and access code has had a human read (if any)
- [ ] No forbidden anti-patterns
- [ ] No stray `console.log`
- [ ] For anything shipped to real users: one goal test probe that proves it still works (see the goal-tests piece of this kit)
- [ ] The human said yes at the checkpoint

---

## Memory rules

- Read the memory index at the start of every session. Open a memory file when its READ BEFORE trigger matches the task.
- One fact per memory file. Update an existing file before creating a new one. Delete memories that turn out to be wrong.
- Never store secrets, client data or anything the repo already records (code, git history).
- At session end, record new decisions and changed project state. Never delete history, only add or correct.
- Open items live in one list with a default each. Never ask the same question twice (see the never-ask-twice piece of this kit).

---

## Deploys and pushes

- No deploy or push without the human's explicit yes, in this session.
- Before: check where it will land (branch, remote, project, URL).
- After: prove it with the real end state (load the live URL, compare local and remote commits). An exit code is not proof.
